SIMULATION HISTORIES:
[
 {
 "simulation_id": "S01",
 "simulation_history": [
 {
 "round": 1,
 "consequence": "No prior action from the coach in this encounter.",
 "world_update": {
 "context": "During an evening when Alex is feeling particularly worn down, the clinic's drop-in remote coaching service is opened from a link in the patient portal. The service is described as something Alex can use from time to time when diabetes feels hard to manage, without any fixed schedule or guarantee of the same coach next time. Recent variability in home glucose readings and a stretch of higher work and financial stress prompted Alex to click the \"Get help with day-to-day diabetes tasks\" button.",
 "resource": "The coaching system retrieves a key clinical summary: Alex is an adult with type 2 diabetes diagnosed 5 years ago, taking metformin and a once-daily long-acting insulin at a dose set by the primary-care clinician. The last few lab results show hemoglobin A1c increasing from 7.6% to 8.3%, and brief chart flags mention variable home glucose readings without recorded severe lows. Alex uses a basic finger-stick glucose meter and usually logs results in a simple phone app that is not connected to the clinic. One close friend occasionally helps with rides and groceries but is not regularly involved in daily diabetes care.",
 "actors": "Feeling skeptical but somewhat desperate for practical tips, Alex opens a new chat session with the coaching service. In intake questions, Alex notes feeling \"pretty stressed\" and \"tired of dealing with this\" and reports that blood sugars have been very up-and-down over the past couple of weeks. Alex indicates checking glucose on most mornings but skipping on some days, and admits to missing several insulin doses on especially hectic or exhausting days."
 },
 "antecedent": "The drop-in coaching platform connects Alex with the digital diabetes coach, displays the brief clinical summary to the coach, and invites Alex to share what has been most challenging lately. Alex sends an initial message expressing frustration with unstable glucose readings and competing life stress, and uncertainty about where to start.",
 "perceived_simulation": "[Messages]\n[system]: You are connected to the clinic's drop-in diabetes coaching chat. This service is for day-to-day questions about managing your existing diabetes plan. Your primary-care clinician still manages your prescriptions and overall treatment. You can return to this service in the future if you find it helpful. Do not use this chat for emergencies—if you have severe symptoms like chest pain, trouble breathing, confusion, or very high or very low blood sugar that won't come up or down, call your clinic or emergency services right away.\n[system]: Brief summary for the coach: Alex is an adult with type 2 diabetes (5 years). Current medications: metformin and a once-daily long-acting insulin at a fixed dose set by the primary-care clinician. Recent A1c has increased from 7.6% to 8.3%. Home glucose readings over the last few weeks have been variable per patient report, with no documented severe hypoglycemia. A recent in-clinic random glucose was moderately elevated. No other major active conditions are highlighted in this summary.\n[patient_Alex]: Hey. I just clicked this because my doctor keeps saying my sugars are creeping up and I saw the \"coaching\" thing in the portal. Not gonna lie, I'm a bit doubtful this chat is going to change much. The last couple weeks my numbers have been all over—some mornings okay, other days higher than they said they want—and with work going nuts and money stress, I keep skipping checks or I realize at night I forgot my insulin. I know that's not great, but I honestly don't know what I'm supposed to focus on fixing first.",
 "response": "You’re not alone—stress makes this harder; let’s focus on two simple, high-impact steps: take your once-daily long‑acting insulin at the same clock time every day, and check your fasting blood sugar each morning for the next 2 weeks. Set a phone alarm labeled “Insulin” at a time tied to a daily habit (when you brush teeth or have coffee), store the pen where you do that habit, and log each morning glucose plus whether you took the shot (phone note or a single paper column is fine). Also add one pre-evening or bedtime check if you can, bring the 2‑week log to your clinician so they can consider adjustments. If you ever miss a dose don’t double up—call the clinic for instructions, and seek urgent care or call the clinic right away if glucose goes above ~300 mg/dL or you develop severe symptoms; what time do you currently take your long‑acting insulin and can you pick a consistent alarm time?"
 },
 {
 "round": 2,
 "consequence": "Your structured advice about choosing a fixed insulin time, doing regular morning checks, logging values, and knowing when to call the clinic gives Alex a clearer sense of priority and slightly increases their willingness to experiment with small changes, though they remain doubtful about how much a chat like this can help.",
 "world_update": {
 "context": "In the last few days, Alex’s glucose readings have stayed variable, with one morning fasting in the low 120s mg/dL, another close to 190 mg/dL, and an after-dinner value yesterday around 250 mg/dL without clear symptoms.",
 "resource": "Alex has started typing a few glucose readings into their phone’s notes app but hasn’t created a consistent log or used any diabetes-specific tracking tool; they have not asked their occasional helper for reminders.",
 "actors": "After reading your message, Alex reflects on their usual evening routine, becomes a bit more forthcoming about their schedule and recent numbers, but continues to express skepticism about whether occasional coaching will lead to real change."
 },
 "antecedent": "Alex answers your question about their insulin timing, shares some recent readings, and asks you to narrow down what they should focus on first.",
 "perceived_simulation": "[Messages]\n[patient_Alex]: So, my long-acting is supposed to be at 10 p.m., but in real life it’s anywhere from like 9:30 to midnight depending on how late I’m working or just zoning out, and a couple nights this week I straight-up forgot it.\n[patient_Alex]: I haven’t set an alarm yet, I just kind of keep thinking “I’ll remember later,” which clearly isn’t going great.\n[patient_Alex]: I did jot a few numbers in my phone: one morning was 123, another was around 185, and last night after dinner it was about 250 — that one stressed me out, but I didn’t feel awful so I just tried to ignore it.\n[patient_Alex]: With work going nuts and money stuff in the back of my mind, it already feels like my brain is maxed out, so adding a bunch of extra tasks sounds nice in theory but I don’t know if I’ll actually follow through.\n[patient_Alex]: Since this is more of a drop-in thing I might or might not come back to, what’s the *one or two* things you’d say are really worth doing right now so it actually moves the needle before my next doctor visit?",
 "response": "Totally get it — with stress and late work you need a tiny set of actions that actually stick, so do just these two things: take your long‑acting insulin at the same clock time every night, and check/record your fasting blood sugar each morning for the next two weeks. Pick one realistic time you can commit to (10:00 pm is fine if you can make it consistent), set a repeating phone alarm labeled “Insulin,” and keep the pen where you do a nightly habit (bedside charger, toothbrush, etc.). Each morning before you eat, do one fingerstick, note the number and whether you took your shot — a quick photo of the meter or a single line in your phone’s Notes works great. If you miss a dose don’t double up—call the clinic for instructions, and seek urgent care or call the clinic if any glucose reading is over ~300 mg/dL or you develop severe symptoms. Can you set the alarm now for 10 pm or tell me what time you’re willing to commit to?"
 },
 {
 "round": 3,
 "consequence": "Having two concrete \"worth it\" actions laid out makes Alex feel a bit more focused and slightly less defensive, though he still doubts he can sustain daily checks amid work and money stress.",
 "world_update": {
 "context": "No context update.",
 "resource": "No resource update.",
 "actors": "Alex thinks through his typical evenings, identifies when he is usually home, and starts weighing a specific alarm time while negotiating with himself about how many morning checks he can realistically manage."
 },
 "antecedent": "Alex sends a follow-up message describing his schedule and recent readings, and asks how much partial follow-through would still be useful before his next doctor visit, noting that he may or may not return to this drop-in service.",
 "perceived_simulation": "[Messages]\n[patient_Alex]: So, most nights I’m back home by around 8:30 or 9, then I’m bouncing between the couch and the laptop until close to midnight.\n[patient_Alex]: I could probably live with a 9:30 or 10 p.m. alarm, I just know myself and I’m worried I’ll be in the middle of work stuff and hit snooze or ignore it.\n[patient_Alex]: When I scroll through the few numbers I bothered to type in this past week, my mornings are anywhere from a bit over 110 up into the 170–180 range, and I’ve seen a couple after-dinner ones in the low 200s, which freaks me out but also makes me want to not look.\n[patient_Alex]: In theory, checking every single morning sounds great, but in reality some days I’m rolling out of bed late and just grabbing coffee and running out the door.\n[patient_Alex]: If I use this as a kind of trial run and actually stick to a 9:45 p.m. alarm and manage, say, a few fasting checks a week instead of every day, is that still enough to give my doctor anything useful to work with, in case I don’t get around to hopping back on this chat again before the visit?",
 "response": "Ideally your clinician gets daily fasting readings for two weeks, but a realistic compromise is fine: set the 9:45 pm alarm and aim for 3–4 fasting checks per week. Try to get at least 6–8 fasting readings total over two weeks plus 1–3 evening/post‑meal checks when you notice high numbers, and always record whether you took your insulin (a photo of the meter + a short note works). To beat snooze, put your phone across the room, label the alarm “Insulin — take now,” and keep the pen with a nightly habit (charger, toothbrush). Don’t double doses if you miss one—call the clinic for instructions—and seek urgent care or call the clinic if any reading goes above ~300 mg/dL or you develop severe symptoms."
 },
 {
 "round": 4,
 "consequence": "Alex feels validated that a limited but consistent effort can still give the clinician useful information and becomes somewhat more willing to try the suggested alarm and monitoring plan, while remaining aware this might be their only drop-in before the next visit.",
 "world_update": {
